        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Headphones</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these headphones. They were chosen from manufacturer
            specifications, store listings, support documentation and independent reviews, and the
            claims on this page are limited to what those sources support. Everything was checked
            on 15 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the designs that suit treadmill running and looked for a current,
            widely sold model in each: open-ear bone conduction, in-ear buds held by an ear-hook,
            in-ear buds held by a wingtip, and budget versions of the ear-hook. For each candidate
            we confirmed the exact current name on the maker's own site or store and on at least one
            major retailer, then checked every figure we wanted to print — the IP rating above all,
            plus battery life, Bluetooth version and fit details — against an independent review or
            a second retailer.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where sources disagreed we left the figure out. That removed more than you might
            expect. JLab's own listing and some retailers give one battery figure for the GO
            Sport+ and another retailer gives a lower one. Soundcore's UK page and its comparison
            table give the Sport X20 an IP68 rating, but its US product page gives no IP figure and
            one retailer lists a different rating. Listings disagree on the weight of the OpenRun
            Pro 2, and on the Powerbeats Fit's battery life with noise cancelling on or off. In
            each case the card says so rather than picking a number.
          </p>
          <p>
            We excluded models that have been replaced or withdrawn. The Beats Fit Pro has been
            succeeded by the Powerbeats Fit, and JLab's GO Air Sport pages now point to the GO
            Sport+. Jabra, whose Elite Active earbuds were long-standing workout picks, announced in
            2024 that it was winding down its consumer earbud lines, so we did not list them. We also
            left out the Shokz OpenFit 2 open-ear buds because the signals on whether that exact
            model is still current were mixed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall listings for
            every brand here. We found no recall of any listed model. The only results were for
            other products from two of the brands: a 2015 recall of a Beats portable speaker, and
            2025 recalls of some Anker power banks and Soundcore Bluetooth speakers. None of those
            are headphones, and none affect the earbuds on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-and-anc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Treadmill Noise and Whether Noise Cancelling Helps</h2>
<p>
            A running treadmill is a noisy room. There is the motor, which produces a steady hum or
            whine that rises with speed; the belt and rollers, which add a constant rushing sound;
            and your footfalls, which arrive as rhythmic thuds that also travel through the frame.
            How loud that is depends heavily on the machine — our guide to
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills</a>
            covers why some are much louder than others — but on almost any treadmill it is louder
            than a quiet road.
          </p>
          <p>
            That matters because the natural response to background noise is to turn the volume
            up. With open-ear headphones, which do nothing to block the room, you are competing
            with the motor directly, and on a loud machine you may find yourself pushing the volume
            further than you would outdoors. That is the main argument for a sealed in-ear design
            on a treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What active noise cancelling does and does not do</h3>
          <p>
            Active noise cancelling, or ANC, works best on steady, continuous sound — exactly the
            kind a motor and belt produce. As a general rule it is less effective on sudden,
            irregular sounds, so expect it to take the edge off the drone and do less about the
            thump of your own feet. Reviewers of the Soundcore Sport X20 describe this pattern
            directly: strong on steady gym and ventilation hum, weaker on sudden clanks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Much of the benefit of a sealed earbud comes before the ANC switches on. A properly
            sized ear tip blocks a good share of the room on its own, which is why the fit section
            below matters as much as the ANC feature list. A sealed pair that fits badly will
            leak noise in and leak bass out, and you will end up turning it up anyway.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The trade-off</h3>
          <p>
            ANC and a tight seal also block the things you might want to hear, which is the subject
            of the next section. Every sealed pair on this page has a mode that lets outside sound
            in — Transparency on the Beats pairs, an ambient mode on the Soundcore, Be Aware mode on
            the JLab — so you can switch between shutting the room out and letting it in. On a
            treadmill in a shared home, that switch is worth more than the strength of the
            cancelling.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever design you choose, keep the volume moderate. A quieter room helps you do
            that more than any headphone feature: a mat will not touch motor noise, but placing the
            treadmill away from hard, echoing walls and maintaining the belt so it runs quietly
            both reduce what you are trying to drown out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="open-ear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Open-Ear Versus Sealed: Hearing the Household</h2>
<p>
            Outdoors, the case for open-ear headphones is traffic. Indoors it is people. If you run
            on a treadmill while children are awake, while you are waiting for a delivery, or while
            someone in the house might need you, open-ear headphones let you hear a call from
            another room without stopping the music.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How the open-ear picks work</h3>
          <p>
            Both Shokz picks are bone-conduction headsets: a band wraps around the back of the head,
            and the transducers rest on your cheekbones in front of the ears, leaving the ear canal
            completely open. You hear the room exactly as you would with nothing on. The
            OpenRun Pro 2 adds a separate air-conduction element for bass, which Shokz calls
            DualPitch. Reviewers generally describe the sound of bone conduction as good for the
            type but not a match for sealed earbuds, particularly in bass, and both Shokz pairs
            leak some sound at higher volumes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The weakness on a treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            Open ears also hear the motor. On a quiet machine at walking pace, that is not a
            problem. On a loud machine at running speed, the motor and belt compete with the music,
            and reviewers note that the OpenRun Pro 2 can be hard to hear in loud places. If your
            treadmill is loud and you find yourself turning open-ear headphones up to compensate,
            a sealed pair with an ambient mode is the better tool.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A simple way to decide</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>You need to hear the house at all times</strong> — open-ear. Choose the
            OpenRun Pro 2 for better sound or the OpenRun for a lower price and a higher water
            rating.</li>
            <li><strong>You run alone, or the treadmill is loud</strong> — sealed with ANC. The
            Powerbeats Pro 2, the Powerbeats Fit or, on a budget, the Soundcore Sport X20.</li>
            <li><strong>Both, at different times</strong> — a sealed pair with a transparency or
            ambient mode, used with the mode switched on when someone might need you.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Open-ear headphones also suit people who find in-ear tips uncomfortable over a long
            session, and they leave nothing in the ear to trap sweat. That last point matters more
            indoors, as the next sections explain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit-at-pace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Staying In at Running Pace</h2>
<p>
            Earbuds that are perfectly secure at walking pace can work loose at running pace. The
            repeated impact of each footfall jolts the bud, and sweat makes the skin and the ear
            tip slippery. On a treadmill there is an extra cost to a bud falling out: it lands on a
            moving belt, gets carried to the back of the deck and can be thrown off or crushed,
            and reaching for it mid-stride is a good way to stumble.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The three ways these pairs stay put</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>A band.</strong> The Shokz headsets do not sit in the ear at all, so they cannot
            fall out of it. The band holds them against the head. The OpenRun and OpenRun Pro 2 are
            both sold in a standard and a Mini size, and choosing the right one matters: a band that
            is too long sits loose and can bounce or drift down the neck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An ear-hook.</strong> A flexible hook over the top of the ear holds the bud in
            place even if the seal loosens. The Powerbeats Pro 2, the Soundcore Sport X20 and the
            JLab GO Sport+ all use one, and the Soundcore's hooks rotate up to 30 degrees and extend
            by 4 mm to fit different ears. Ear-hooks are the most secure in-ear design for running,
            but some people find them uncomfortable over long sessions, especially with glasses,
            and reviewers of the Sport X20 report that the hooks can cause pressure for some
            wearers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A wingtip.</strong> A soft fin on the bud tucks into the ridge of the outer ear
            and braces it. The Powerbeats Fit uses this design; Beats made its wingtip more
            flexible than the Beats Fit Pro's and describes the hold as comparable to an ear-hook.
            Reviewers who ran in them report that they stayed secure. Wingtips leave nothing behind
            the ear, which suits glasses wearers, but their hold depends more on the shape of your
            outer ear than a hook does.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Get the tip size right</h3>
          <p>
            For any in-ear design, the ear tip does two jobs: it seals out noise and it grips the
            ear canal. Try every size in the box, and do not assume the medium tips fitted at the
            factory are yours. Apple notes for the Powerbeats Pro 2 that the tip size that seals
            best for sound may differ from the one that gives the best heart-rate reading. A
            sensible test is a few minutes at your normal running pace before you rely on them for
            a long session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweat-and-ip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Sweat, IP Ratings and Looking After Them</h2>
<p>
            Indoor running usually feels sweatier than outdoor running at the same effort,
            because there is no moving air to carry sweat away. Headphones sit right where it runs, so water
            resistance is not a nice extra. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-stay-cool-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">staying cool on a treadmill</a>
            covers the fan and room changes that reduce the problem at source.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Reading the IP code</h3>
          <p>
            An IP rating has two digits. The first is protection against dust and solids, the
            second against water; an X means that part was not rated. On this page:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>IPX4</strong> (both Beats pairs) means protection against splashing water
            from any direction. Apple describes these as sweat and water resistant for non-water
            sports.</li>
            <li><strong>IP55</strong> (OpenRun Pro 2, JLab GO Sport+) adds dust protection and
            protection against low-pressure jets of water.</li>
            <li><strong>IP67</strong> (OpenRun) means dust-tight and able to survive temporary
            immersion, which reviewers describe as up to a metre for 30 minutes. It is not a
            swimming rating.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a treadmill, IPX4 is the practical minimum and anything higher is extra margin.
            Two details are easy to miss. Apple says the Powerbeats Pro 2 charging case is not
            water resistant, while the Powerbeats Fit case is rated IPX4; and both Apple and Shokz
            warn that water resistance is not permanent and can diminish with wear.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">After every session</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Wipe the earbuds or band with a dry, soft cloth before they go back in the case.</li>
            <li>Let them dry fully before charging, especially pairs with charging contacts.</li>
            <li>Rinse or replace silicone ear tips periodically; sweat and skin oil build up on
            them.</li>
            <li>Do not leave them in a closed gym bag overnight with damp clothes.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The same discipline protects the treadmill. Sweat dripping onto the console and the
            motor hood is a common cause of corrosion, and a towel over the rail does more for the
            machine than it looks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cables-and-bluetooth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Cables, the Safety Key and Bluetooth Range</h2>
<p>
            Every pick on this page is wireless, and on a treadmill that is more than convenience.
            A wired headphone cable running from your ears to a phone in the console tray is a loop
            waiting to catch something — the handrail, your swinging arm, or the safety key cord
            clipped to your clothing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why the safety key matters here</h3>
          <p>
            The safety key cord is meant to pull free and stop the belt if you drift back or fall.
            Anything that tangles with it can either pull the key out when you did not mean to stop,
            or, worse, stop it pulling freely when you do need it. Clip the cord to your waistband,
            not to a pocket where a cable or a phone sits, and keep everything else clear of it. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a>
            cover how to wear the key properly, and why it should always be attached.
          </p>
          <p>
            Even a wireless neckband or the cord on some sport buds can swing, so tuck anything
            loose under your shirt. And if a bud falls out, let it go: stop the belt, then pick it
            up. Do not reach down while the belt is moving.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bluetooth range and where to put the phone</h3>
          <p>
            Most sports headphones are specified for a range of roughly 10 metres, or about 33
            feet, in open air. Shokz quotes that for the OpenRun Pro 2, and JLab quotes 30 feet or
            more for the GO Sport+. In practice range is shorter through bodies and walls, and
            signal drops are more likely when the phone is behind you or in a pocket on the
            opposite side from the earbud that handles the connection.
          </p>
          <p>
            The simple fix on a treadmill is to put the phone in the console tray or on a holder in
            front of you, where it is within a metre and in line of sight. Some treadmill consoles
            can also pair with Bluetooth headphones directly to play their own audio; check your
            manual, because console Bluetooth audio varies a lot between machines. If you also
            watch video on a tablet, multipoint pairing — supported on the OpenRun Pro 2 — lets
            the headphones stay connected to two devices at once.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Heart rate from the earbuds</h3>
          <p>
            The Powerbeats Pro 2 has a built-in optical heart-rate sensor. Apple says it can share
            readings with Apple Fitness, the Health app, compatible third-party apps and some
            compatible gym equipment; RTINGS describes it as handy but not a replacement for a
            dedicated monitor. If you train to heart-rate zones, our guide to
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate monitors for the treadmill</a>
            explains why a chest strap is still the more dependable choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you only walk.</strong> At walking pace on a walking pad, almost any
            comfortable wireless earbuds stay in. You do not need ear-hooks or a sport design; the
            buds you already own are probably fine, and the money is better spent elsewhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you watch screens while you run.</strong> If the treadmill runs a streaming
            or class app, check whether the console supports Bluetooth audio and how it handles
            lip-sync before buying headphones for it. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmill-workout-apps/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workout apps</a>
            covers the apps themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you swim as well.</strong> None of these pairs is sold for swimming. The IP67
            OpenRun survives immersion but is not designed for laps; a dedicated swimming headset
            is the right purchase for that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your treadmill is very loud.</strong> Before spending more on noise
            cancelling, check whether the machine needs attention. A belt that needs lubricating or
            adjusting, a worn roller or a loose part can make a treadmill much noisier than it
            should be, and fixing that helps everyone in the house, not just you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are kitting out a new treadmill.</strong> Headphones are a personal
            choice; a mat, the right lubricant and a fan are not. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill accessories guide</a>
            covers the items that protect the machine and make sessions bearable, and our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmill-fans/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill fans</a>
            covers the one that does most for the sweat problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the best headphones for treadmill running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on whether you need to hear the room. If you do, open-ear bone-conduction headphones such as the Shokz OpenRun Pro 2 leave your ears free. If you want to block the motor, sealed earbuds with an ear-hook and noise cancelling, such as the Beats Powerbeats Pro 2, suit most runners. On a budget, the JLab GO Sport+ stays in at a low price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do noise cancelling earbuds block treadmill noise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They reduce it. Active noise cancelling works best on steady, continuous sound, which is what a motor and belt produce, so it takes the edge off the drone. It does less for the thud of your footfalls. A well-fitted ear tip does much of the work on its own, so try every tip size in the box before judging the noise cancelling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are bone conduction headphones good for the treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are good when you need to hear the household, because they leave your ears completely open and cannot fall out onto the belt. Their weakness indoors is that they do nothing to block motor noise, so on a loud treadmill you may need more volume than is comfortable. On a quiet machine or at walking pace they work well.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop earbuds falling out when running on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a design with an ear-hook or wingtip, and try every ear tip size until one seals and grips firmly. Test them at your normal running pace for a few minutes before a long session. If a bud does fall out, stop the belt before picking it up; never reach down to a moving belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What IP rating do I need for workout earbuds?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For treadmill running, IPX4, which covers splashing water from any direction, is a sensible minimum. IP55 and IP67 give more margin for heavy sweat. No rating makes earbuds suitable for swimming unless the maker says so, and makers note that water resistance can diminish over time, so wipe and dry them after every session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I connect Bluetooth headphones to my treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some treadmill consoles can pair with Bluetooth headphones to play their own audio, and many cannot; check the manual for your model. Most people pair the headphones to a phone or tablet in the console tray instead. Keeping the device in front of you, within a metre or so, avoids most dropouts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing Headphones for the Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a sensible purchase into one that is uncomfortable, unsafe or simply too quiet to enjoy.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running with a wired headset</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A cable looped from your ears to a phone in the console tray can catch the handrail, your arm or the safety key cord. That can pull the key when you did not mean to stop, or stop it pulling freely when you need it. Use wireless headphones, tuck anything loose under your shirt, and clip the key cord to your waistband.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying open-ear for a loud machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Open-ear headphones are excellent when you need to hear the house, but they do nothing to block a loud motor. If you find yourself turning them up to hear over the belt, they are the wrong tool for that treadmill. A sealed pair with an ambient or Transparency mode lets you choose when to hear the room.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sticking with the factory ear tips</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The tips fitted in the box are just a starting point. A tip that is too small leaks noise and bass, so you turn the volume up, and works loose with sweat; a tip that is too big gets pushed out. Try every size, test at running pace, and replace tips that have gone slick or lost their shape.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging them straight after a sweaty run</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sweat left on charging contacts and in the case is an avoidable way to shorten the life of workout earbuds, and makers note that water resistance diminishes with wear. Wipe the buds or band dry after every session, let them air before they go into the case or onto the charger, and do not leave them in a damp gym bag.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

